From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HFM <hfm@ocp>
Date: Thu, 16 Jul 2026 18:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] subsys/pmci/mctp: clear the MCTP tag-owner bit on control
 responses (auto-discovery fix)

Fix mctpd's automatic endpoint discovery (SetupEndpoint) against the Zephyr
serial_bridge node. The control-protocol responder replied to Get/Set
Endpoint ID with MCTP_MESSAGE_TO_SRC, i.e. tag-owner (TO) bit = 1 on the wire.
An MCTP *response* must clear TO: the requester (the BMC bus owner, mctpd) owns
the message tag (DSP0236 8.1), so the reply must reuse the requester-supplied
tag with TO = 0.

Why TO = 1 broke discovery on the real BMC:

  * mctpd enumerates a static endpoint with physical addressing (null EID)
    over a dedicated query socket (endpoint_query_addr). The Linux kernel
    AF_MCTP stack matches the reply to that waiting socket by (src, dest, tag),
    and the match key includes the TO bit (net/mctp/route.c mctp_lookup_key:
    tag = flags_seq_tag & (MCTP_HDR_TAG_MASK | MCTP_HDR_FLAG_TO)).
  * A response carrying TO = 1 fails that key match, so the kernel treats the
    frame as a *fresh request* and routes it to mctpd's bound daemon socket
    (mctp_lookup_bind) instead of the query socket. mctpd logs "Got control
    request command code 2" and the query socket never sees the answer, so
    SetupEndpoint times out and no endpoint is published on D-Bus.

This is the mirror image of the requester-path fix (requests must set TO = 1,
see patch 0009): responses must clear it.

With this change, on a kernel whose AF_MCTP dump is not broken (patch 0010)
and with the serial link ordered before mctpd (patch 0006), mctpd's
SetupEndpoint completes, adopts EID 18, auto-installs the kernel route and
neighbour, and publishes endpoints/18 so pldmd's platform-mc polls it. No
manual "mctp route add" is required.

Signed-off-by: HFM <hfm@ocp>
---
 subsys/pmci/mctp/mctp_control.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/subsys/pmci/mctp/mctp_control.c b/subsys/pmci/mctp/mctp_control.c
index 9e478dfa5f4..87d3e65b506 100644
--- a/subsys/pmci/mctp/mctp_control.c
+++ b/subsys/pmci/mctp/mctp_control.c
@@ -56,6 +56,17 @@ LOG_MODULE_REGISTER(mctp_control, CONFIG_MCTP_LOG_LEVEL);
 /*
  * Fill the control-response header into @buf and transmit @len bytes (message
  * type + control header + payload) back to @src. The caller fills the payload.
+ *
+ * A control *response* must clear the MCTP tag-owner (TO) bit: the requester
+ * (the BMC bus owner, e.g. mctpd) owns the tag, so we reply with TO=0 reusing
+ * the requester-supplied @msg_tag. mctpd runs Get/Set Endpoint ID with
+ * physical addressing (null EID) on a dedicated query socket and matches the
+ * reply by (src,dest,tag), where tag includes the TO bit (see route.c
+ * mctp_lookup_key). Replying with TO=1 makes the BMC kernel treat the frame as
+ * a fresh request and deliver it to mctpd's bound daemon socket instead of the
+ * waiting query socket, so endpoint discovery (SetupEndpoint) times out. This
+ * is the mirror image of the requester path, which must set TO=1 (see the
+ * reverse PLDM probe in the serial_bridge sample).
  */
 static void ctrl_send(struct mctp_control_ctx *ctx, mctp_eid_t src,
 		      uint8_t msg_tag, uint8_t iid, uint8_t cmd, uint8_t *buf,
@@ -68,7 +79,7 @@ static void ctrl_send(struct mctp_control_ctx *ctx, mctp_eid_t src,
 			    (iid & MCTP_CTRL_HDR_INSTANCE_ID_MASK);
 	buf[OFF_CMD_CODE] = cmd;
 
-	rc = mctp_message_tx(ctx->mctp, src, MCTP_MESSAGE_TO_SRC, msg_tag, buf,
+	rc = mctp_message_tx(ctx->mctp, src, MCTP_MESSAGE_TO_DST, msg_tag, buf,
 			     len);
 	if (rc != 0) {
 		LOG_WRN("control cmd 0x%02x tx failed: %d", cmd, rc);
--
